The Philosophical Framework of the Post-Perception Movement:From Plato to Artificial Intelligence
Prepared by: Ibrahim Shalabi
Conceptual Artist and Founder of the "Post-Perception" Movement
Introduction: Perception as a Central Philosophical Issue
Human perception has long been one of the most complex philosophical dilemmas throughout history. How do we know the world? Can our senses be trusted? What is the relationship between what we see and what truly exists? These questions have gained newfound urgency in the era of digital simulation and Artificial Intelligence, where images are generated by algorithms rather than reality, and deception has become so persuasive that discernment is nearly impossible.
The "Post-Perception" movement is the legitimate offspring of a long evolution in philosophy and art, stretching from Plato to Phenomenology, and from Arab-Islamic heritage to contemporary simulation theories. This article explores the philosophical framework upon which the movement is built, tracing the evolution of the concept of perception through history and demonstrating how the movement transcends these philosophies to provide a response to an unprecedented perceptual crisis.
