I. Roots of the Matter: Perception in Greek Philosophy
1. Plato: Perception as a Shadow of Truth
Plato (427–347 BC) represents the starting point for questioning human perception. In his dialogue The Republic, specifically the famous "Allegory of the Cave," Plato presents a radical vision: humans live as prisoners in a cave, seeing nothing but the shadows of objects projected onto a wall, believing these shadows to be reality itself. The sensible world, perceived through the senses, is but a reflection of the World of Forms—the true realm of absolute ideas.
For Plato, the senses are deceptive and unreliable because they are limited to perceiving changing material phenomena, while truth lies in the Forms, which can only be grasped by the intellect. The eye can see a physical tree, but it cannot perceive the absolute "Form of the Tree." This skepticism is the first seed of the critique of perception that culminates in the Post-Perception movement. If Plato doubted the senses' ability to reach absolute truth, Post-Perception doubts their ability to reach any truth in an age where simulation matches—or even surpasses—the original.
2. Aristotle: Perception as a Bridge to Knowledge
In contrast to his teacher, Aristotle (384–322 BC) offered a more positive view. In De Anima (On the Soul), he argues that the senses are the primary gateway to knowledge. The cognitive process begins with sensory perception, which provides the mind with the data necessary to form concepts.
To Aristotle, perception is not inherently deceptive; rather, it is a tool that can be refined by reason to reach the truth. However, Aristotle's assumption that the senses can be reliable remains limited, as it overlooks the complexities of cultural, psychological, and technological influences. Here, Post-Perception intervenes to reveal that the senses, even in their simplest operations, are subject to shaping, direction, and manipulation.
