II. Modern Philosophy: From Doubt to Construction
3. Descartes: Methodological Doubt and the Mind as a Filter
In the 17th century, René Descartes (1596–1650) introduced a paradigm shift through his methodological doubt. In Meditations on First Philosophy, he questioned the reliability of the senses, arguing they could be deceptive, as seen in dreams or optical illusions. His "Evil Demon" hypothesis suggests that our senses might mislead us entirely, and that the mind alone—through logical reasoning—is the reliable source of knowledge. The Cogito ergo sum ("I think, therefore I am") establishes the centrality of the mind in the perceptual process.
4. Kant: Perception as a Mental Structure
With Immanuel Kant (1724–1804) in The Critique of Pure Reason, we witness a radical transformation. Kant argues that perception is not a passive reception of sensory data, but an active process shaped by the structures of the mind (such as Space and Time as forms of sensibility). Perceived reality (Phenomena) is the product of the interaction between the senses and the mind, while absolute truth (the Thing-in-itself or Noumena) remains outside human reach.
Post-Perception builds on this legacy but transcends it: if Kant showed that the mind constructs reality, the movement reveals that this very structure is vulnerable to hacking and manipulation in the algorithmic age.
