III. Phenomenology: The Return to Sensory Experience
5. Husserl: Intentionality and the Phenomenological Bracketing
At the turn of the 20th century, Edmund Husserl (1859–1938) founded Phenomenology as a method to understand perception by returning to "the things themselves." He called for the Epoché (bracketing)—suspending prior judgments about the existence of the external world to focus on the phenomenon as it appears to consciousness.
6. Merleau-Ponty: The Body as the Center of Perception
Maurice Merleau-Ponty (1908–1961) shifted phenomenology toward the body. In Phenomenology of Perception, he argues that perception is not an abstract mental process but a bodily experience. The "Living Body" (Le corps vécu) is our means of being in the world. This view deeply influenced contemporary installation and interactive art, providing fertile ground for Post-Perception, which integrates multiple senses to deconstruct perception.
