IV. Arab-Islamic Philosophy: A Parallel Path
7. Al-Ghazali: The Betrayal of the Senses
Al-Ghazali (1058–1111) offered a unique contribution to the critique of perception. In The Incoherence of the Philosophers and Deliverance from Error, he embarked on a journey of methodological doubt centuries before Descartes. He noted how the eye sees a mirage as water or a star as a tiny point when it is actually a giant. Post-Perception finds an intellectual ancestor in Al-Ghazali; if he asked "Can the senses err?", the movement adds: "How do we protect ourselves from this error when deception is technologically magnified?"
8. Ibn Sina (Avicenna): Internal Powers and Degrees of Perception
Ibn Sina presented an integrated model of perception in The Book of Healing, categorizing it into:
•	Sensory Perception: Receiving data via the five senses.
•	Imaginative Perception: Storing and recalling sensory images.
•	Estimative Perception (Wahm): Perceiving non-sensory meanings (like love or enmity).
•	Intellectual Perception: Abstracting meanings from matter to reach universals. This reinforces the idea that perception is a complex, multi-layered process.
9. Other Arab Philosophers
•	Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen): Provided a scientific analysis of visual deception in Book of Optics.
•	Ibn Tufail: Showed in Hayy ibn Yaqdhan that perception can be formed internally through self-reflection.
•	Ibn Arabi: Emphasized "unveiling" (Kashf) as a path to knowledge that transcends both reason and sense.
