V. Post-Structuralism and Simulation: The Contemporary Context
10. Michel Foucault: The Power of Discourse
In This is Not a Pipe, Foucault argues that perception is a discursive process subject to cultural and historical power. We see what we are programmed to see.
11. Jean Baudrillard: Simulation and the Death of the Original
Baudrillard’s theory of "Simulacra and Simulation" is a cornerstone for Post-Perception. He argues that the image no longer represents reality but precedes it (Hyperreality). In the third stage of simulation, the image masks the absence of any profound reality. This "Neo-Platonism" in the AI era is evident in the "Platonic Representation Hypothesis," where AI models converge toward a shared statistical "ideal" of reality.
